Negative control — `findOne`'s NESTED-PARTIAL containment (AC-7, design D-11,
hazard Z1).

Mutates the collection context's `findOne:` member to expose `findOneByValue` —
the shared `useCollection` helper — in place of the local `findOne` that wraps
it. The wrapper is what deep-partial-matches nested plain-object values; the
shared helper compares each mapped top-level key with a strict `isEqual`, so
`{ address: { city } }` can never match the full mapped `address` object. The
member stays present and still resolves string lookups identically, since the
wrapper delegates those to the very helper this mutant substitutes.

The mutation reinstates the platform hazard rather than inventing one: this is
`useCollection`'s real behaviour, and it is why the containment is contained
LOCALLY here (mirroring `client-phone/useClientPhones.context.ts:49-68`) with
the SHARED helper deliberately not edited.

Declared AC — AC-7: "`findOne` matches a nested partial" —
`findOne({ address: { city: "<fixture city>" } })` returns the matching row.

No collateral measured. String-mapping lookups route to the same helper either
way, and `ensure`'s whole-object match is the degenerate full case, so nothing
outside AC-7's read-backs observes the difference.

